    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. In most of these agreements I'm seeing, the language is somewhat vague about what constitutes a \"beyond control\" event. If a tenant claims that disruptions to their supply chain or unexpected government mandates fall under this, how do courts usually interpret the threshold for \"unforeseeability\"?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Courts typically impose a higher threshold for \"unforeseeability\" under a force majeure clause, requiring that the event was so unusual or unpredictable that the parties could not reasonably have anticipated its occurrence. Disruptions to supply chains or unexpected government mandates may not automatically qualify as a force majeure event, as these are often considered predictable risks, especially for commercial ventures. In such cases, the court may scrutinize the language of the clause and the parties' intent to determine whether the exclusion of such risks was reasonable and reasonably anticipated.",
      "source": "target",
